 ## Background
-The method was developed as part of interactive volume rendering research using multi-dimensional transfer functions [S2, S3]. It was implemented on SGI Octane 2 with V series graphics cards and nVidia GeForce3 series graphics adaptors [S3]. The approach addresses the lack of shading in previous texture-memory based volume rendering work [S2]. The concept of packing sample points into a 2D texture for volume ray tracing was also explored in the context of gradient-based interpolation and sampling for real-time rendering of inhomogeneous, single-scattering media [S1].
+The method was developed as part of interactive volume rendering using multi-dimensional transfer functions. It was implemented on the SGI Octane 2 with V series graphics cards and the nVidia GeForce3 series graphics adaptor [S3]. The approach addresses the lack of shading in previous texture-memory based volume rendering work, aiming to improve visual quality without significant performance penalties [S2]. The specific implementation details regarding `glCopyPixels()` and scratch buffers are tied to the SGI Octane2 platform's rasterization capabilities [S3].
 
